WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:35.000
This is Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedanthan. In the early 1990s, Jim Heckman traveled to Corpus Christi, Texas. He was there to see something that had been described to him as a miracle. It was a program for students who had dropped out of high school. And they said, you know, we have a program which will take high school dropouts, people who had dropped out at 9th grade or before actually, and converted them into high school graduates within 2-3 months in our program, and then we send them out to the workplace.
这里是《隐藏的大脑》，我是尚卡尔·维丹塔姆。20世纪90年代初，吉姆·赫克曼前往德克萨斯州的科珀斯克里斯蒂。他去那里是为了见证一件被描述为“奇迹”的事情。那是一个针对高中辍学生的项目。他们说：“我们有一个项目，可以接收高中辍学生，实际上是那些九年级或更早辍学的人，在我们的项目中，他们能在2到3个月内成为高中毕业生，然后我们把他们送入职场。”

2
00:00:35.000 --> 00:01:15.000
So I said, well, this is amazing. The program that gave young people a second chance is now known widely across the country. It's called the GED. Which was a program which students studied for our test, and at the end of the test, they were certified as legitimate high school graduates. There were plenty of individual success stories, but Jim is an economist, and he wanted to know whether on average people with a GED were as successful as those with a high school diploma. He started by looking at their test scores. What really surprised me was their test scores
所以我说，这太神奇了。这个给年轻人第二次机会的项目现在在全国广为人知。它被称为GED（普通教育发展证书）。这是一个学生通过学习来参加考试的项目，考试结束后，他们会被认证为合法的“高中毕业生”。虽然有很多个人的成功案例，但吉姆是一位经济学家，他想知道，平均而言，拥有GED证书的人是否和拥有高中毕业证书的人一样成功。他首先查看了他们的考试成绩。真正让我惊讶的是他们的考试成绩

3
00:01:15.000 --> 00:02:05.000
were virtually the same. Within a few months the GED program was allowing students to catch up with peers who had spent years in high school. But then Jim looked further, were GED grads holding on to jobs and long-term relationships, staying out of trouble? If you looked at the GEDs, you realized they weren't doing so well. They were likely, it turned out, if they got a job, to quit the job or be fired. If they went into marriage or some partnership, how stable were those compared to the ordinary high school graduates? Much less stable. What about their crime? Much more likely to commit crime. What was going on? Why would GED recipients struggling if they had the same test scores as high school graduates?
几乎是一样的。在短短几个月内，GED项目就让学生赶上了在高中学习多年的同龄人。但随后吉姆进行了更深入的研究：GED毕业生是否能保住工作、维持长期关系、不惹麻烦？如果你观察GED毕业生，你会发现他们表现得并不好。事实证明，如果他们找到工作，他们很可能会辞职或被解雇。如果他们结婚或建立某种伴侣关系，与普通高中毕业生相比，这些关系有多稳定？非常不稳定。犯罪率呢？犯罪的可能性要高得多。到底发生了什么？为什么GED获得者在考试成绩与高中毕业生相同的情况下，生活却如此挣扎？

4
00:02:05.000 --> 00:03:06.000
It was kind of a revealing insight that these people were as smart as high school graduates measured by the kind of test that so much public policy focuses on, but in real life they were really not succeeding in any real way. This week on Hidden Brain untangling the mystery Jim Heckman discovered in Corpus Christi and the astounding implications his findings have for public policy. James Heckman is a professor at the University of Chicago. In 2000, he won the Nobel Prize in Economics. Some of his early work focused on programs like the GED that are designed to give young people a second chance. Jim found that these programs were succeeding
这是一个颇具启发性的洞察：这些人以公共政策所关注的那种测试来衡量，和高中毕业生一样聪明，但在现实生活中，他们并没有取得任何真正的成功。本周的《隐藏的大脑》将解开吉姆·赫克曼在科珀斯克里斯蒂发现的谜团，以及他的发现对公共政策产生的惊人影响。詹姆斯·赫克曼是芝加哥大学的教授。2000年，他获得了诺贝尔经济学奖。他早期的一些工作专注于像GED这样旨在给年轻人第二次机会的项目。吉姆发现这些项目是成功的，

5
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:34.000
and getting young people to do well in tests, but failing when it came to improving their outcomes in life. It prompted him to ask broader questions about the American educational system. I realized that the whole educational establishment, at least the establishment that looked at test scores and that valued schools and valued people by these test scores really was just missing important dimensions of human behavior.
让年轻人在考试中表现良好，但在改善他们的生活结果方面却失败了。这促使他提出了关于美国教育体系更广泛的问题。我意识到整个教育机构，至少是那些关注考试成绩、以这些成绩来评价学校和人的机构，确实忽略了人类行为的重要维度。

6
00:03:34.000 --> 00:04:03.000
And so that brought me onto a subject which has fascinated me and what I think is really important for success and understanding success and failure. And that is the notion of what I call non-cognitive skills. By non-cognitive skills, I met skills that weren't measured by these tests. And what I found, and that surprised me, was that those non-cognitive skills were extremely important. And then when you think about it, you say, well, what an idiot. Of course, these are important.
这让我进入了一个让我着迷的主题，我认为这对于理解成功与失败至关重要。那就是我所说的“非认知技能”的概念。所谓非认知技能，我指的是那些无法通过这些测试衡量的技能。我发现，令我惊讶的是，这些非认知技能极其重要。当你仔细思考时，你会说，真是个笨蛋。当然，这些很重要。

7
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:40.000
And then you go back to everything you ever heard from the time you were a child, the Esaubs Fable, the tortoise in the hair, and Teddy Roosevelt saying, you know, it's the man of grit, and the person who would go down, who gets back up again and tries, and you know, all of the motto as we quote to our children and that many presidents and leaders have told us for centuries, I mean, it's in many religious teachings. So I said, you know, this is obviously true, but it turned out that in psychology at that time, there was a sense that somehow personality
然后你回想起你从小到大听到的所有东西，伊索寓言，龟兔赛跑，还有西奥多·罗斯福说的，你知道，是那种有毅力的人，那种跌倒后能爬起来继续尝试的人，你知道，我们引用给孩子的所有座右铭，以及许多总统和领导人几个世纪以来告诉我们的，我的意思是，这在许多宗教教义中都有体现。所以我说，你知道，这显然是真的，但事实证明，当时的心理学界有一种感觉，认为人格

8
00:04:40.000 --> 00:05:15.000
and the subject of personality was kind of, you know, a dark and unrigorous corner. Whereas the IQ test, there we actually had machinery to measure it. We had to waste a score test and so forth and so on. So what I did then is that started a lifetime investigation on the social and emotional skills. And there's evidence, by the way, that staying in school, that working through will build those skills. In 2014, the makers of the GED overhaul the exam partly in response to the work of Jim
以及人格这个课题，在某种程度上是一个黑暗且不严谨的角落。而智商测试，我们确实有测量它的机器。我们有韦氏智力测验等等。所以我所做的是，开启了一项关于社会和情感技能的终身调查。顺便说一句，有证据表明，坚持上学、努力工作会培养这些技能。2014年，GED的制定者部分为了回应吉姆·赫克曼和其他研究人员的工作，对考试进行了彻底改革。

9
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:45.000
Heckman and other researchers. A spokesman for the GED program says the new test more effectively measures problem-solving skills, critical thinking, and writing. It also demands more from test takers by way of preparation and grit. To put it another way, the GED now does more to tap into non-cognitive skills. Jim says these kinds of skills can be summed up with a single word. Character.
GED项目的一位发言人表示，新测试能更有效地衡量解决问题的能力、批判性思维和写作能力。它还通过准备工作和毅力对考生提出了更高的要求。换句话说，GED现在更多地挖掘了非认知技能。吉姆说，这类技能可以用一个词来概括：品格。

10
00:05:45.000 --> 00:06:12.000
One time in American education. I mean, I'm talking a long time ago when Horace Mann was devising the first common schools for all Americans, character training was literally part of the curriculum. I think Horace Mann has a quote in the 1840s when he was setting up this movement. And basically he said something that says it all really, you know, that reading, writing, and arithmetic
在美国教育史上曾有过一段时期。我的意思是，很久以前，当贺拉斯·曼为所有美国人设计第一批公立学校时，品格训练实际上是课程的一部分。我想贺拉斯·曼在19世纪40年代发起这场运动时有一句名言。基本上，他说了一些真正概括了一切的话，你知道，阅读、写作和算术

11
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:52.000
is about a small portion of what we teach in school. And then he goes on to say that what we're really teaching is character. We're teaching children how to just stay on task, to control their emotions, to work with others, to persist, and so this whole bundle of attributes, we know, are important in life, and the common school was designed basically to foster all of those. A clear example of character education was the hugely popular textbook series called McGuffee Readers. You'd find them in many classrooms between the mid-19th century and the mid-20th century.
只是我们学校教授内容的一小部分。然后他继续说，我们真正教授的是品格。我们教孩子们如何保持专注、控制情绪、与他人合作、坚持不懈，所以我们知道，这一整套属性在生活中很重要，而公立学校的设计初衷基本上就是为了培养所有这些。品格教育的一个明显例子是广受欢迎的教科书系列《麦加菲读本》。在19世纪中期到20世纪中期，你会在许多教室里找到它们。

12
00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:23.000
The textbooks were filled with stories about how to live a virtuous life. This one is about a hen who works hard to take care of her 12 chicks. She asks them to follow her as she jumps across a brook to a stone, but they don't obey. Oh mother, we can't, we can't, we can't! Said all the little chickens. Yes, you can, if you try," said the old hen, just flap your wings as I did and you can jump over. Only one of the chickens, Chippy, even tries.
这些教科书里充满了关于如何过上道德生活的故事。这一篇是关于一只母鸡努力照顾她的12只小鸡的故事。她让它们跟着她跳过小溪去一块石头上，但它们不听。 “哦，妈妈，我们不能，我们不能，我们不能！”所有的小鸡都说。“是的，你们能，只要你们尝试，”老母鸡说，“像我一样拍打翅膀，你们就能跳过去。”只有其中一只小鸡，奇皮，尝试了。

13
00:07:23.000 --> 00:08:00.000
When they got home, the old hen began to look about for something to eat. She soon found near the back door a piece of bread. So she called the chickens and they all ran up to her. Each one trying to get a bite at the piece of bread. No, no, said the old hen. This bread is for Chippy. He is the only one of my children that really tried to jump to the stone. One of the most interesting is the story about a couple of boys who ran away from school, played hooky, stole a boat, and then the ocean came up and they were nearly drowned, and finally,
当它们回到家时，老母鸡开始寻找吃的。她很快在后门附近发现了一块面包。于是她叫来小鸡，它们都跑向她。每一只都试图咬一口面包。 “不，不，”老母鸡说，“这块面包是给奇皮的。它是我的孩子中唯一真正尝试跳到石头上的。”最有趣的故事之一是关于两个逃学、旷课、偷了一艘船的男孩，后来海浪涌上来，他们差点淹死，最后，

14
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:39.000
by the grace of God, they were thrown onto the beach and saved, and they learned their lesson, and they never ran away from school again, and they did their lessons. So there were a lot of stories like that. Unfortunately, from the standpoint of some elements of society, and I really mean it was unfortunate, those early lessons were more or less using the Bible, the Protestant Bible, as the touchstone to teach morality and character. And then, of course, other groups, as they came to the United States, more Catholics, for example, Jews, and other groups.
靠着上帝的恩典，他们被冲上海滩获救了，他们吸取了教训，再也没有逃学，并完成了他们的功课。所以有很多这样的故事。不幸的是，从社会某些阶层的角度来看，我确实认为这很不幸，那些早期的课程或多或少地使用《圣经》，即新教《圣经》，作为教授道德和品格的试金石。当然，随着其他群体来到美国，例如更多的天主教徒、犹太人和其他群体，

15
00:08:39.000 --> 00:09:19.000
As they came, they found this very offensive. They didn't want to get their children indoctrinated. And so that aspect of character gradually got edited out of the common school. And then psychology, meanwhile, had this quote, development, this great breakthrough that was really cognitive psychology, that really said IQ and cognition and pure problem solving ability, that was really the be all and end all of an education. And we shouldn't worry about this. Touchy, feely, stuff, building Americans, learning to work with others, understanding democracy.
当他们到来时，他们觉得这非常冒犯。他们不想让自己的孩子被灌输。因此，品格的那一方面逐渐从公立学校中被删除了。与此同时，心理学有了所谓的“发展”，这种伟大的突破实际上是认知心理学，它真正宣称智商、认知和纯粹的解决问题能力，才是教育的全部和终极目标。我们不应该担心这些感性的东西，比如培养美国人、学习与他人合作、理解民主。

16
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:46.000
All of that was viewed as junk. And it really hit the trash can in the late 50s when Sputnik surprised Americans. And literally, if you read the education journals of the time, what really was needed was get your math right, get your science right, and the rest of it was kind of taken for granted. So this is fascinating, because what you're pointing out is that for a long time, the
所有这些都被视为垃圾。在50年代末，当苏联发射人造卫星让美国人感到震惊时，这些东西真的被扔进了垃圾桶。如果你阅读当时的教育期刊，你会发现真正需要的是搞好数学，搞好科学，其余的都被认为是理所当然的。所以这很迷人，因为你指出的是，长期以来，

17
00:09:46.000 --> 00:10:15.000
link between character and what was taught in school had a religious component to it. And as the country became perhaps more secular or more diverse, people had some resistance to bringing those religious ideas along. But of course, you don't need religion to teach questions related to character. Modern psychology has done a lot of thinking about some of these traits. What traits are we talking about when we use the term character? What kind of traits are these? Well, there's a range of traits.
品格与学校教育之间的联系带有宗教成分。随着国家变得更加世俗化或多元化，人们对引入这些宗教思想产生了一些抵触。但当然，你不需要宗教来教授与品格相关的问题。现代心理学对其中一些特质进行了大量思考。当我们使用“品格”这个词时，我们在谈论什么特质？这些是什么样的特质？嗯，有一系列特质。

18
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:57.000
What I mean is the ability to plan. How well can I actually plan my life forward? Make good use of time. How well can I persist on a task? And how resilient am I? If I experience failure, how far can I go and bounce back and try, try again? How open am I to experience? So, in other words, am I closed off? Do I just have to shun contact with new ideas and with new people? How agreeable am I in a sense of, you know, I may find somebody disagrees with me, but that doesn't mean I have to fight them. It means I listen, they listen to me,
我的意思是规划能力。我能多好地规划我未来的生活？善用时间。我能在任务上坚持多久？我的韧性如何？如果我经历失败，我能走多远并反弹回来，再次尝试？我对经验的开放程度如何？换句话说，我是封闭的吗？我是否必须回避与新思想和新人的接触？我在某种意义上有多随和，你知道，我可能会发现有人不同意我的观点，但这并不意味着我必须与他们争斗。这意味着我倾听，他们倾听我，

19
00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:34.000
and we can agree, and we can also agree to disagree. But there's a sense of having a sense of propriety about the relationship. So this term character is a quite broad term, really. It'll be very elastic in some ways. One of the most important qualities that Jim identified is conscientiousness, or what the psychologist Angela Duckworth calls grit. Jim also found that the ability to delay gratification matters a lot. In other words, I can see again if I wait today, like go to school, it's unpleasant, I don't like
我们可以达成一致，也可以同意保留不同意见。但有一种对关系保持礼仪的感觉。所以“品格”这个词确实是一个非常广泛的术语。在某些方面它非常灵活。吉姆确定的最重要的品质之一是尽责性，或者心理学家安吉拉·达克沃斯所说的“毅力”。吉姆还发现，延迟满足的能力非常重要。换句话说，如果我今天等待，比如去上学，虽然不愉快，我不喜欢

20
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:58.000
the teacher, I don't like studying, but I know that if I finish these two years, I will have much better opportunity in life. So I control myself and stay in school. You know, I grin and bear it, I guess, is the way to describe it. So I think it's this ability to manage yourself and to work with others. It's both. Do you think this is true for even Nobel Prize winners?
老师，我不喜欢学习，但我知道如果我完成了这两年，我的人生将会有更好的机会。所以我控制自己并坚持上学。你知道，我想“咬牙坚持”就是描述它的方式。所以我认为这是管理自己和与他人合作的能力。两者兼而有之。你认为这对诺贝尔奖得主也适用吗？

21
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:27.000
I mean, when you look at your own life in career, do you see sort of the role of character of perseverance and conscientiousness and grit as in some ways being just as important or perhaps more important than cognitive and intellectual abilities? Oh, for sure. And the reason why I say for sure is take some truly important scientists like Marie Curry, her work for example, in isolating radium, taking
我的意思是，当你审视自己的人生和职业生涯时，你是否认为品格、毅力、尽责性和韧性的作用在某些方面与认知和智力能力一样重要，甚至更重要？哦，当然。我之所以说“当然”，是因为看看一些真正重要的科学家，比如玛丽·居里，例如她分离镭的工作，在一百多年前巴黎的一些棚屋后面蒸馏

22
00:12:27.000 --> 00:13:06.000
pitch blend or distilling it in the back of some sheds in Paris back more than a hundred years ago. That was just brutal. Gruntworks, she had an idea for sure, but it required that she stay with it and stay with it for years. Take all of the rocket scientists, many failures to say, get a satellite into orbit or to get a rocket to fly. I think in every case, especially in science, but especially in learning, literally, you have to stay with it. And it's one of the loneliest things in the world, the original research.
沥青铀矿。那简直是残酷的苦差事。她当然有想法，但这需要她坚持下去，坚持多年。看看所有的火箭科学家，为了让卫星进入轨道或让火箭飞行，经历了多少次失败。我认为在每一个案例中，特别是在科学领域，但尤其是在学习中，你确实必须坚持下去。这是世界上最孤独的事情之一，原创性研究。

23
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:25.000
Bertrand Russell in his autobiography has a great line. It was a very young man, a brilliant guy, a acknowledged genius. And he described how every morning he would go to his desk, and there was a sheet of paper. And he was trying to prove something. It turned out what he was really trying to prove was impossible.
伯特兰·罗素在他的自传中有一句很棒的话。他当时是一个非常年轻的人，一个聪明绝顶的人，公认的天才。他描述了他每天早上如何走到书桌前，那里有一张纸。他试图证明某件事。结果证明他真正试图证明的是不可能的。

24
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:49.000
So the story is even better in that regard. But he went there and he said he would look at this paper all day and then he would go home or go back to his bed and sleep without having written a line on it. And he did this for weeks, sometimes months, because he was waiting for a breakthrough. His mind was working, but he couldn't write anything down.
所以在这个方面，这个故事甚至更好。但他去了那里，他说他整天盯着这张纸，然后回家或回到床上睡觉，连一行字都没写出来。他这样做持续了数周，有时是数月，因为他在等待突破。他的大脑在运转，但他写不出任何东西。

25
00:13:49.000 --> 00:14:28.000
So I really is a question of staying with it. And that's why Einstein said it, Edison said it. Genius is like 1% inspiration, a 99% perspiration. I mean, there are some people who strike it rich. I mean, you fall down and you see, oh my God, I stubbed my toe on a piece of gold and it goes down one mile and you're rich or something. But that's pretty unusual. Pretty unusual. You know, we had Angela Duckworth on Hidden Brain maybe a year or two ago and she told me something really interesting during that conversation. She brought up something the German philosopher Nietzsche had said about how we all in some
所以这确实是一个坚持下去的问题。这就是为什么爱因斯坦说过，爱迪生说过。天才就是1%的灵感，加上99%的汗水。我的意思是，有些人确实发了大财。我的意思是，你摔倒了，你看到，哦天哪，我的脚趾踢到了一块金子，它延伸了一英里，你就发财了之类的。但这非常罕见。非常罕见。你知道，我们一两年前在《隐藏的大脑》中采访过安吉拉·达克沃斯，在那次谈话中她告诉我一些非常有趣的事情。她提到了德国哲学家尼采说过的话，关于我们所有人都在某种

26
